First, we expand the parentheses:

$$
\begin{aligned}
& -2\cos \theta\sin \theta\left(\frac{\cos \theta}{\sin \theta}+\frac{\sin \theta}{\cos \theta}\right) \\ &= -2\cos \theta\sin \theta\cdot\frac{\cos \theta}{\sin \theta} \\ &\quad {}-2\cos \theta\sin \theta\cdot\frac{\sin \theta}{\cos \theta}
\end{aligned}
$$

Next, we group the $\sin^2 \theta$ and $\cos^2 \theta$ terms and simplify any matching factors.

$$
\begin{aligned}
& -2\cos \theta\sin \theta\cdot\frac{\cos \theta}{\sin \theta} \\ &\quad {}-2\cos \theta\sin \theta\cdot\frac{\sin \theta}{\cos \theta} \\ &= -2(\cos^{2} \theta+\sin^{2} \theta)
\end{aligned}
$$

Then, we use $\sin^2 \theta+\cos^2 \theta=1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:

$$
\begin{aligned}
& -2(\cos^{2} \theta+\sin^{2} \theta) \\ &= -2
\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore, we conclude that

$$
\begin{aligned}
& -2\cos \theta\sin \theta\left(\frac{\cos \theta}{\sin \theta}+\frac{\sin \theta}{\cos \theta}\right) \\ &= -2.
\end{aligned}
$$

